IP Address Configuration:
-------------------------
    (PC0)
    IP Address      : 192.168.10.10 (identity of PC0)
    Subnet Mask     : 255.255.255.0 (this tells PC0 which devices belong to my local network)
                                    (So, PC0 knows, anyone between 192.168.10.1 and 192.168.10.254 is in local network)
    Default Gateway : 192.168.10.1 (the exit gate from local network)
                                   (whenever PC0 wants to communicate with another network, it sends the packet to this gateway)

    (PC1)
    IP Address  : 192.168.20.20
    Subnet Mask : 255.255.255.0
    Default Gateway : 192.168.20.1

    Why the Gateway different:
    ---------------------------
    Because PC1 belongs to a different network Each network must have its own gateway

Q) PC0 wants to ping 192.168.10.50: (will not send to default gateway)
----------------------------------------------------------------------

    1) PC0 checks its own network:
    ------------------------------
        PC0 has: IP address 192.168.10.10
        Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0

        So PC0 knows: 192.168.10.0/24
        Now the destination is: 192.168.10.50
        PC0 compares: My network 192.168.10.0, Destination 192.168.10.0
        They are the same network, So, PC0 immediately says: I don't need the router

    2) Then what happens:
    ---------------------
        PC0 send an ARP Request,
        It asks: who has 192.168.10.50
        The ARP Request ues the broadcast MAC = FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF
        The switch floods the ARP request
        If PC 192.168.10.50 exists, It replies with its MAC address
        Then PC0 sends the data directly, The router is never involved

    * PC0 sends the packet directly to 192.168.10.50, Because both devices are in the same subnet
    * The default gateway is not used

Q) PC0 wants to ping 192.168.20.20: [will send to Default Gateway]:
-------------------------------------------------------------------
    - It send to the default gateway,
      Because PC0 compares: My network 192.168.10.0, Destination 192.168.20.0

    Different Networks:
    -------------------
    So PC0 says: I cannot reach this network directly

    Then:
    ----
    1. ARPs for the default gateways MAC address (if not already cached)
    2. Sends the Ethernet Frame to the Router's MAC address
    3. Keeps the destination IP as 192.168.20.20

Q) When is the Default gateway used:
------------------------------------
    - The default gateway is used only when the destination IP address belongs to a different network,
    - If the destination is in same subnet, the source device communicates directly by resolving
      destination device's MAC address using ARP

Q) Suppose PC0 (192.168.10.10/24) wants to communicate with 192.168.10.200. Will it use the default gateway:
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    - PC0 first compares the destination IP with its own subnet using the subnet mask,
    - Since 192.168.10.200 belongs to the same 192.168.10.0/24 network,
    - PC0 does not use the default gateway, instead, it sends an ARP request to learn the MAC address of
      192.168.10.200 and then sends the Ethernet Frame directly to the device
      















































